==English==
{{was wotd|2021|January|3}}

===Etymology===
Probably a reference to the passing of as many [[Sunday]]s as there are [[day]]s in a [[month]], that is, between 28 and 31 [[week]]s.<ref>Compare {{R:Phrase Finder|entry=A month of Sundays|url=https://www.phrases.org.uk/bulletin_board/59/messages/130.html}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˌmʌnθ əv ˈsʌndeɪz/|/-diz/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˌmʌnθ əv ˈsʌnˌdeɪz/|/-ˈsən-/|/-diz/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-month of Sundays.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|month of Sun|days}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[month]] [[of]] [[Sunday]]s|months of Sundays}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic|informal}} A very [[long#Adjective|long]] [[time#Noun|time]]; a [[period]] [[regard#Verb|regarded]] as too long. {{defdate|from mid 18th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|eternity|forever|q2=both [[hyperbolic]]|week of Saturdays|week of Sundays|q4=both informal|Thesaurus:eon}}
#: {{ux|en|It seems like it’s been a '''month of Sundays''' since we saw him last.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The life and real adventures of Hamilton Murray|page=121|author=Hamilton Murray|year=1759|passage={{...}} which increased his agony so intolerably, that he exhibited the various contorsions and wreathings of a rattle snake with surprizing velocity, to the no small edification of the commander, who swore he should dance to the second part of the same tune, for a '''month of Sundays'''; still repeated the word citation, which he could neither understand, nor digest.}}

====Usage notes====
The term is often used negatively, for example, “[[never in a month of Sundays]]” or “not in a month of Sundays”.

====Derived terms====
